
Diabetes Data Analysis Project
Date:
11 October 2026

Project:
Diabetes Data Analysis — Medallion Architecture (Fabric Lakehouse + Power BI)

1. What I Planned to Do
My goal was to complete a full end‑to‑end data engineering and analytics workflow using Microsoft Fabric and Power BI. Specifically, I wanted to:

Upload and manage data in a Fabric Lakehouse

Build Raw → Silver → Gold layers using medallion architecture

Clean and transform the dataset using Python

Connect Power BI Desktop to the Gold layer

Build a clinically meaningful dashboard

Apply conditional formatting and interactive slicers

Produce insights that reflect real healthcare analytics

This project was designed to strengthen my skills in Fabric, Power BI, and modern data engineering patterns.

2. What I Actually Did
I successfully completed the entire workflow:

Fabric Lakehouse
Created a Lakehouse and uploaded the raw Pima Indians Diabetes dataset

Built Silver and Gold layers using Python transformations

Stored cleaned and curated data in Parquet format

Power BI Desktop
Connected directly to the Gold layer using the Lakehouse connector

Built KPI cards for BMI and Glucose

Created BMI Categories and Glucose Bands using DAX

Added slicers for Age, BMI Category, Glucose Band, and Outcome

Designed bar charts, pie charts, and scatter plots

Applied conditional formatting to highlight clinical risk

Added an “About the Data” section and a professional subtitle

Dashboard Enhancements
Improved layout and readability

Added colour-coded clinical indicators

Ensured the dashboard tells a clear analytical story

3. What Went Well
I fully understood and applied the medallion architecture

My Fabric Lakehouse transformations worked smoothly

Power BI visuals were clear, meaningful, and clinically relevant

Conditional formatting added strong visual impact

The final dashboard looks professional and portfolio-ready

I gained confidence connecting Fabric → Power BI Desktop

4. What Didn’t Go Well
I initially struggled with the Direct Lake connector

I repeatedly hit the “Premium workspace required” error

I spent time in Power BI Online instead of Desktop

The wrong connector kept opening, causing confusion

I had to learn the difference between Direct Lake vs Lakehouse connectors

These challenges slowed me down but taught me valuable lessons.

5. What I Learned
Direct Lake requires Premium — not suitable for my workspace

Power BI Desktop is essential for Lakehouse connections without Premium

How to structure data using medallion architecture

How to create DAX measures for conditional formatting

How to design dashboards that communicate clinical meaning

How to troubleshoot Fabric → Power BI connectivity issues

How to build a complete analytics project from scratch

This project significantly improved my confidence with Fabric and Power BI.

